# SDDRA Git Workflow

Purpose:
  Git workflow conventions for the SDDRA system.
  For branch naming and hierarchy, see @branches/INDEX.sdd.
  This file defines commit conventions, hooks, and workflows only.

Owns: GIT-WORKFLOW.sdd

## Semantic Commits

Format: `<type>(<scope>): <subject> [DEC:<id>] [TASK:<id>]`

Types:
  feat:     New feature for the user
  fix:      Bug fix for the user
  docs:     Documentation changes
  style:    Code style changes (formatting, no logic change)
  refactor: Code refactoring (no feature change, no bug fix)
  test:     Adding or updating tests
  chore:    Maintenance tasks (deps, build, etc.)
  build:    Build system changes
  ci:       CI/CD changes
  perf:     Performance improvements
  revert:   Revert previous commit

Scopes (SDDRA-specific):
  sdd:      .sdd/ system files
  project:  project/ source code
  docs:     documentation
  chains:   chain graph and execution
  skills:   engineering skills
  prompts:  prompt processing
  commands: CLI commands
  templates: project templates
  patterns: resilience patterns
  workflows: execution workflows
  decisions: decision ledger
  gates:    quality/security gates
  tests:    test files

Examples:
  feat(auth): add login endpoint [DEC:DEC-001] [TASK:TASK-AUTH-001]
  fix(chains): resolve D0 loop issue [DEC:DEC-002] [TASK:TASK-CHAIN-001]
  docs(readme): update getting started

Breaking Changes:
  feat(sdd)!: redesign chain graph
  fix(api)!: change endpoint signature
  chore(deps)!: upgrade major version

Breaking change indicator: `!` before `:`

Commit Body:
  Optional detailed explanation
  Wrap at 72 characters
  Explain WHAT and WHY, not HOW

Commit Footer:
  BREAKING CHANGE: <description>
  Reviewed-by: <name>
  Co-authored-by: <name> <email>

## Git Hooks

### commit-msg

Validates commit messages follow semantic format.

Location: .git/hooks/commit-msg

Validation:
  - Must match: `<type>(<scope>): <subject>`
  - Type must be one of: feat, fix, docs, style, refactor, test, chore, build, ci, perf, revert
  - Subject must be present
  - Breaking changes must include footer

### pre-commit

Runs checks before commit.

Checks:
  - Lint .sdd/ files
  - Validate .sdd/ references
  - Check for secrets
  - Run tests (only changed files)

### pre-push

Runs checks before push.

Checks:
  - Run full test suite
  - Validate chain graph
  - Check documentation

## Recovery

### Recovering from Bad Commit

1. Revert commit:
   git revert <commit-hash>

2. Or reset (if not pushed):
   git reset --hard HEAD~1

3. Fix and recommit:
   git commit -m "fix(sdd): corrected issue"

### Aborting Merge

git merge --abort

### Aborting Rebase

git rebase --abort

## Best Practices

1. Commit often, commit early
2. Write meaningful commit messages
3. Keep commits atomic (one change per commit)
4. Never commit secrets or credentials
5. Never commit generated files
6. Review diffs before committing
7. Sign commits for important changes
8. Use tags for releases

